  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=\\pi \n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\pi .$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\pi ,$ we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is indeed a point of inflection.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
